<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Cefalù Cathedral</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1131</span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Cefalù Cathedral is a Norman cathedral in the town of Cefalù on the north coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a>, founded by Roger II in 1131. Its massive twin-towered front and the Byzantine mosaics of its apse, dominated by a great figure of Christ Pantocrator, are celebrated works of Norman Sicilian art. It is part of the World Heritage Site Arab-Norman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palermo">Palermo</a> and the Cathedral Churches of Cefalù and Monreale.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cefalu-cathedral.jpg" alt="Cefalù Cathedral" width="320" height="296" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: User:Matthias Süßen · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Duomo_cefalu_msu2017-0797.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
